Formatting commentary with Claude...

  (commentary generated by claude-fable-5)
Energy is the morning's story: Brent and WTI are both off nearly 2% (86.6 and 81.0) after reports of "deep talks" between Washington and Tehran, with the pause in US–Iran hostilities pulling the geopolitical risk premium out of crude for a second session. The offset is OPEC+, where delegates say the group plans to halt quota hikes after a final September increase while it assesses the supply fallout from the Iran conflict — a floor-building signal that should limit how far the front end of the curve unwinds. TTF and Henry Hub followed crude lower, and softer energy fed straight into a bond bid, with Treasuries and Bunds firmer across the strip even as a Reuters poll of economists flagged persistently sticky global inflation.

Risk tone is split rather than sour. A deepening chip slump has money rotating out of AI infrastructure into earnings winners — hence Nasdaq futures down 0.7% while the Dow rallies nearly 1% and the S&P holds flat — a classic rotation, not a de-risking. The bigger macro overhang is the Fed, which meets this week with markets still mulling residual hike odds; the dollar has firmed to a one-month high, and that's the pressure sitting on the metals complex — gold, silver, platinum and aluminium all down 1%-plus, with real-rate and dollar headwinds doing the damage rather than any demand signal.

In ags, arabica coffee ripped 4.3% to record territory as traders digested the withdrawn US tariff threats against Colombia, while cotton and the livestock complex sold off hard. Watch the Fed decision, Iran headlines, and whether the chip rotation stabilizes index breadth or broadens into genuine risk-off.

